  "substantive_findings": [
    "892: q03 correctly distinguishes D=-56 from formal S=8/3,P=10/3. Claiming those ratios as real-root invariants would be false. All eight existence/invariant tasks remain.",
    "893: q04-q07 keep complete root-plus-invariant records. Matching both invariants is mathematically valid by the selected converse theorem, but does not establish independent written solving followed by a separate check.",
    "894-896: paired unknowns and reconstructed root pairs retain sum/product normalization, exact decimals and the difference conditions. q12-o3 and q13-o4 show that correct roots alone cannot establish a record with the wrong product sign.",
    "897: q14-o1 has the correct sign conclusion but falsely states P=-7 instead of -7/3. The stem requires correct numerical premises too; it is independently false, not silently accepted because its conclusion agrees.",
    "898: q16 preserves c=7-5sqrt(2)<0, proved exactly by 50>49. The positive discriminant alone cannot justify two positive roots; III has opposite signs.",
    "899: all eight root pairs have a polynomial and factor/square equivalence with a recognizable solve-back step. Nonzero-scalar normalization is allowed. The supplied right representations reduce production capacity but still require expansion, equivalence and return-root judgment.",
    "900: q22 and q23 are not interchangeable because 3x+1 and 3(x+1) differ. q25 is an identity on all real numbers, including -3; inability to cancel a zero factor does not exclude that value.",
    "901: all three distinct contradictions remain. q26-o3 fails cancellation, o4 replaces square zero by a half-line, and o5 uses OR instead of the conjunction required by the absolute-value bound."
  ],
